  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 AM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Much colder air arrives today and persists into\nearly next week, bringing widespread sub-freezing temperatures \ninland tonight. A Freeze Warning is in effect for portions of \nthe area tonight into Saturday morning, with near- freezing \ntemperatures possible again Sunday morning.\n\nEarly morning analysis shows deepening low pressure E-SE of \nEastern Long Island, and a 1028+ mb high building across the \nupper Midwest. Aloft, a potent upper trough digs over the \neastern CONUS. This feature will linger over the Mid-Atlantic \nthrough the weekend before weakening early next week, ushering \nin the coldest airmass of the season so far. Strong cold air \nadvection will drive breezy NW winds today and Saturday, with \ngusts of 20-25 mph inland and 25-30 mph along the coast peaking \non Saturday. Nudged high temperatures down a bit over the \nEastern Shore, as a developing deformation zone keeps \nconsiderable wraparound low- mid level cloud cover over the \nDelmarva today. While this feature should stay far enough to our\nNE to keep precipitation out of the area, overcast skies should\nstill hold highs in the 40s for much of the Eastern Shore and \nno higher than the lower 50s over the VA Northern Neck. Clouds \nwill scatter out farther south, allowing highs to reach the mid-\nto- upper 50s south of I-64 under a partly cloudy sky. Highs \nreturn to the 50s area- wide this weekend into early next week, \nwith dry weather still prevailing.\n\nAside from lingering clouds over the Delmarva, clear skies, a \ndry airmass, and diminishing inland winds tonight will promote \nefficient radiational cooling and the first widespread freeze of\nthe fall. Lows are forecast to drop into the upper 20s to low \n30s inland, with upper 30s to low 40s near the coast. \nAccordingly, the Freeze Watch has been upgraded to a Freeze \nWarning for the I-95 corridor across central and south-central \nVA into Hertford and Northampton counties in NC. While a better-\nmixed boundary layer Saturday night should keep temperatures \nslightly higher, additional Freeze Watches may be needed for \nSunday morning in counties that do not experience a freeze \ntonight. Lingering breezes will push wind chills into the mid- \nto-upper 20s inland (mid 30s at the coast) for those early \nrisers this weekend.",
